<h2 id="w3">5. W3: one class per forward hides decode work in serving</h2>
<div class="quote"><div class="who">Wade, on <code>h/tron/models/model.hpp:1746</code></div>
<p>Design question on the forward classification. It is per forward: one token job without a listener moves the whole forward, including all its decode attention, into <code>prompt_or_mixed</code>. Under rinzler continuous batching, most forwards that carry any prefill chunk are mixed, so <code>decode_like</code> would show only the pure-decode forwards and misattribute most decode attention. The runtron validation can't show this because prefill and decode forwards are separate there.</p>
<p>Was per-forward granularity chosen on purpose? A per-q_batch class, or tagging each token job by whether it has a listener, looks nearly free: it could be hoisted once per <code>apply_page_range</code> call like <code>stats_enabled</code>. It would answer "how much <em>decode</em> attention went to AMX/FPGA" in serving. If per-forward is intended, can the README spell out that <code>decode_like</code> means pure-decode forwards only?</p></div>

<h3>In plain words</h3>
<div class="plain">
<p><strong>The class.</strong> Every forward gets one label, its <em>forward class</em>: <code>decode_like</code> when every token job of the forward has a listener, <code>prompt_or_mixed</code> otherwise. All counters of the forward are filed under that label. A <em>listener</em> is the scheduler entry that receives the logits (per-word scores) of one token job. A generated token always has one. A prompt chunk (one slice of a prompt, 128 tokens by default, the unit in which a prompt enters a forward) has one, on its last token only.</p>
<p><strong>The problem Wade sees.</strong> In serving, rinzler runs <em>continuous batching</em>: a new request joins the running batch at the next forward. So one forward can hold a prompt chunk of one user next to the generated tokens of seven other users. That forward has jobs without a listener. Its class is <code>prompt_or_mixed</code>. The seven users' decode work (decode = one new generated token per user per forward, after the user's prompt is already in the KV cache) is then counted as prompt-or-mixed work. runtron (our test tool) starts all users together. So it never produces such a forward, and a runtron run could not show this problem.</p>
<p><strong>What holds and what does not.</strong> Wade's description of the mechanism holds. His first idea, a class per q_batch (the code name of a <em>minibatch</em>: a group of token jobs the forward processes together), does not work: minibatches mix users too. His second idea, one tag per token job, works. The information exists before the counters start. <code>has_listener</code> (model.hpp:1801-1803) is computed for one assert and then discarded when <code>gather_token_job_ids</code> returns. Option C keeps one byte per job, filled from <code>listener_job_index</code> (model.hpp:1116, :1794-1797). Each visit then reads that byte at est. 2 to 4 cycles (options table below).</p>
<p><strong>The answer.</strong> Per forward was a deliberate choice, made on 2026-09-25 for the reasons on record. The per-job tag was not considered then. For this PR: the README sentence Wade asks for, plus two counters per class (token jobs with and without a listener). With those two counters a reader sees how mixed the forwards were. The per-job split of the visit counters is proposed as a follow-up PR, unless you want it in this one.</p>
