<h2 id="w5">7. W5: rows keyed by attention worker, not pool worker</h2>
<div class="quote"><div class="who">Wade, on <code>h/tron/models/self_attention.hpp:498</code></div>
<p>Design question on row sizing. Rows and <code>path_bits</code> are sized by <code>attn.size()</code>, the whole app pool (27 in your run), but rows are indexed by <em>attention</em> worker index (0..<code>n_attn_workers</code>), 20 in your run. So: <code>&lt;class&gt;_worker_&lt;W&gt;</code> leaves exist for indices that never do attention; <code>active_workers</code> works around that. <code>end_forward</code> loops over all pool workers per token job, on the main thread between <code>plugin_state.run</code> and <code>wait_for_coop_gof</code>. If <code>n_attn_workers</code> changes between forwards, attention worker <code>W</code> maps to a different pool thread (<code>num_workers() - n_attn_workers + W</code>), so a per-worker row mixes cores over the run.</p>
<p>Was keying by attention worker index (instead of pool worker index) deliberate? Keying by pool worker index would make per-worker rows stable per thread/core, and the fold could skip workers with no bits set in the forward. Alternatively, record <code>n_attn_workers</code> per forward so readers can interpret the rows.</p></div>

<h3>What the code does</h3>
<p>Each forward, the llama plugin (the model-specific code that drives one forward) asks <code>recommend_n_main_helpers</code> how many pool workers help the main thread. The rest become attention workers. Attention worker <code>i</code> is launched on pool worker <code>n_main_helpers + i</code> [h/tron/plugins/llama.hpp:592-609].</p>
{pre('''      const size_t n_main_helpers = outer_state.recommend_n_main_helpers();
      const size_t n_main_workers = 1 + n_main_helpers;
      const size_t n_attn_workers = tp.num_workers() - n_main_helpers;
      outer_state.assemble_attention_plan(n_attn_workers);
      ...
      for (size_t i = 0; i < n_attn_workers; ++i)
        tp.enqueue_to(
            n_main_helpers + i, [this, &q_batches, &workers_done, i, n_attn_workers]() {
              run_attention_worker(q_batches, i, n_attn_workers);''')}
<p class="ev">llama.hpp:593-609.</p>
<p>The helper count comes from one of four branches [model.hpp:1994-2027]:</p>
<ul>
<li>a default of (min + max) / 2 while the time estimators (running averages of main-thread and attention time) have no data,</li>
<li>a fixed split under <code>pay_for_determinism</code> (the run flag that trades speed for repeatable output),</li>
<li>the same fixed split for a single-minibatch forward,</li>
<li>the ratio of the two time estimators, which are refreshed every forward.</li>
</ul>
<p>A fifth outcome, zero helpers when the maximum is 0, cannot fire with 27 workers. The result is then clamped to the largest minibatch's token count [model.hpp:2029-2037]. With 27 pool workers: min 1, max 13, default 7, fixed split 27 x 2 / 7 = 7.</p>
<p>The rows are sized by the pool [self_attention.hpp:480, :496-498] but written under the attention worker index: <code>run_sections</code> passes <code>worker_ix</code> as the scratchpad argument [self_attention.hpp:1171-1173], and the three hooks use it [self_attention.hpp:855, :1315, :1652]. <code>run_attention_job</code> already computes the pool index for the speedometer (tron's per-thread speed record) [self_attention.hpp:797, :837]. The perfetto argument "app thread id" computes it per <code>apply_page_range</code> call [self_attention.hpp:1463-1465]. So tron's own per-thread records key by pool worker. The stats are the exception. The comment at :1649-1651 records the reason: the scratchpad index is the only index the visit loop holds, and it gives one writer per row.</p>

<div class="fig">{W5_SVG}
<p class="cap">Figure 13. The measured 1-user run: the first prompt forward uses 7 main helpers (attention worker 0 = pool worker 7), and every 1-token decode forward is clamped to 1 helper (attention worker 0 = pool worker 1). Today both write row 0. The union of indices ever used is the reported <code>active_workers</code> 26.</p>
</div>

<h3>Verdict on each claim</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rows and <code>path_bits</code> are sized by the pool, 27. <span class="pill holds">holds</span> Every exit report reads <code>"n_workers":27</code>.</li>
<li>Rows are indexed by attention worker, 20. <span class="pill holds">holds, and 20 is per run</span> The six 8-user FPGA runs and the two 8-user warm runs read <code>active_workers</code> 20. The eight 1-user runs read 26.</li>
<li>Worker leaves exist for indices that never do attention. <span class="pill holds">holds</span> 7 leaves per class read zero in an 8-user run.</li>
<li><code>end_forward</code> loops over all pool workers per job on the main thread. <span class="pill holds">holds</span> Cost of that loop, measured on a desktop CPU with the loop copied into a small program (two independent reproductions): about 0.1 us at 8 jobs, 6 to 10 us at 1,024 jobs, 50 to 80 us at 8,192 jobs, 27 workers. Against the measured forward walls of the 8-user FPGA run (7.8 ms per decode forward, 394 ms per 1,024-job prompt forward) that is under 0.003 %.</li>
<li>If <code>n_attn_workers</code> changes between forwards, a row mixes threads. <span class="pill holds">holds, and it happens inside one run</span> In the 1-user runs the first 128-token prompt forward takes the default 7 helpers (20 attention workers). That forward has 2 minibatches, so later prompt forwards use the estimator branch. Every 1-token decode forward takes the single-minibatch fixed split 7, clamped to 1 helper by the 1-token minibatch (26 attention workers). Attention worker 0 is pool worker 7 in one class and pool worker 1 in the other. The 8-user decode forwards are Insufficient data. Every 8-user run (3 CPU, 6 FPGA, 2 warm) reads <code>active_workers</code> 20. A reading of <code>chunk_evenly</code> (the function that splits jobs into minibatches) and the clamp predicts two minibatches of 4 jobs, and so at least 23 attention workers. No per-forward record exists, the run logs carry no minibatch or split line, and no campaign flag explains it. The <code>n_attn_workers</code> min/max leaf below, or one debug line per forward, resolves it.</li>
<li>Was attention-index keying deliberate? <span class="pill partly">for one property only</span> Single writer per row. No reader-facing reason was recorded. Pool keying keeps the single-writer property, because the mapping is one-to-one inside a forward.</li>
<li>Pool keying would make rows stable per thread. <span class="pill holds">holds</span> The pool starts one thread per worker index for the life of the pool and pins it at start [h/tron/threading.hpp:762-767, :819-822].</li>
<li>Record <code>n_attn_workers</code> per forward. <span class="pill holds">holds, but the hook point is not <code>begin_forward</code></span> <code>begin_forward</code> runs before the plugin picks the split. <code>assemble_attention_plan_impl</code> [model.hpp:2280] is the plugin-independent main-thread point that knows it before the workers launch.</li>
</ul>

